   "action": "Run twice \u2014 markdown (no flag) and --json \u2014 and search stdout+stderr concatenated for the sentinels.",
   "expected": "rc 0 both. Neither 'sk-SENTINEL-ONE' nor 'sk-SENTINEL-TWO' appears anywhere in either output: the row reports only the truthiness of env values. The 'qwen config (model, provider)' detail is exactly 'default sentinel-model, auth test-oauth-type, 0 listed models, endpoint sentinel-host.invalid, key set in settings env (file claim; key validity not tested)' with status reported and proof the path \u2014 the presence of the model name, auth type and endpoint proves the file WAS parsed, so a green sentinel check is not the result of a silent parse failure. Only the host segment of baseUrl is shown (no path/query), so a token smuggled into the URL tail stays hidden.",
   "can_fail_because": "Turn 'key = \"key set in settings env\" if any(env.values()) else \u2026' into 'key = f\"env {env}\"' (or append 'json.dumps(d)' to the detail, the classic debug leftover) \u2192 SENTINEL-ONE leaks and both modes go red. A quieter mutation: 'any(env.values())' \u2192 'all(env.values())' \u2192 the empty-valued EMPTY key makes the phrase 'no key in settings env' \u2192 the detail assertion red while the secret check stays green, which is exactly the difference the two assertions are there to separate."
  },
  {
   "id": "RT-06",
   "name": "--require exit-code matrix, dedupe, and the 'inventory reports, require gates' rule",
   "setup": "bin/ with only two fake executables, 'git' and 'python3', both printing a version and exit 0; no other binaries on PATH. HOME=tmp, cwd=tmp proj.",
   "action": "Five runs: (a) --require git ; (b) --require nope ; (c) --require git --require nope ; (d) no --require ; (e) --require git --require git --json.",
   "expected": "(a) rc 0, git row verified. (b) rc 1, a Tools row item 'nope' status MISSING detail 'not on PATH' proof '`command -v nope`' \u2014 a --require'd name that is absent is also *checked*, because gather appends args.require to the binary list. (c) rc 1: a single missing required tool fails the whole gate. (d) rc 0 even though claude/codex/agy/qwen/node rows are all MISSING \u2014 the script only gates what was explicitly asked for; an inventory that always failed would be useless as a preflight. (e) exactly one Tools row for 'git' and 7 Tools rows total (dict.fromkeys dedupe), rc 0.",
   "can_fail_because": "In exit_code, drop the 'return 1' for the required-missing branch (or 'continue' instead) \u2192 (b) and (c) exit 0 red. The opposite mutation, 'return 1 if missing' (fail on any MISSING row regardless of --require), makes (d) exit 1 red \u2014 pinning that the gate is opt-in is as important as pinning that it fires."
  },
  {
   "id": "RT-07",
   "name": "--require against a binary that exists but exits non-zero (present wart)",
   "setup": "bin/broken: a script printing 'boom on stderr' to stderr and exiting 3; everything else fake-or-absent, HOME=tmp.",
   "action": "Run 'readiness.py --require broken --json'.",
   "expected": "The Tools row 'broken' has status 'unknown', detail 'exit 3: boom on stderr' (stdout empty, so run_cmd falls back to stderr, first line only), proof '`broken --version`'. rc is **0**, recorded as the script behaves today: exit_code only fails on MISSING, so a present-but-broken tool satisfies --require even though --help promises 'tool that must be verified present'. The test name says WART so nobody mistakes the green for an approval; if EJ tightens --require to verified-only, the expectation flips to rc 1 (and RT-10's hanging tool joins it) \u2014 that edit must come with a note in DECISIONS.md, not silently.",
   "can_fail_because": "In check_binary, treat any rc that is not None as success (delete the 'if rc == 0' branch) \u2192 the row becomes verified with the wrong detail and this test goes red while the exit-code half stays green \u2014 which is why the case asserts both the status/detail and rc. The tightening mutation ('if c.status != VERIFIED: return 1' inside the requires loop) turns rc 1 and is the other half of the tripwire."
  },
