   "can_fail_because": "Make 'cell(text)' return text unchanged (drop .replace('|', '\\\\|')) \u2192 the claude row yields 5 pipe-separated cells and the 'splits into 4 cells' assertion red \u2014 a table that silently shifts columns is the failure mode worth a test for. Alternatively 'out.splitlines()[0][:100]' \u2192 '[:300]' \u2192 the codex length assertion red."
  },
  {
   "id": "RT-10",
   "name": "A hanging binary hits CMD_TIMEOUT instead of hanging the inventory",
   "setup": "bin/claude whose whole body is 'exec sleep 60' (exec, so the SIGKILL subprocess.run sends on timeout reaps the sleeper itself and leaves no wrapper shell); the other 6 DEFAULT_BINARIES absent so their rows resolve instantly. HOME=tmp, --require claude.",
   "action": "Run with a wall-clock measurement around subprocess.run (test-level timeout well above the bound).",
   "expected": "rc 0 (an UNKNOWN row never trips the gate \u2014 see RT-07). The claude row has status 'unknown', detail 'no answer within 10s', proof '`claude --version`'. 10s \u2264 elapsed < 25s: the lower bound proves the timeout actually fired rather than a fast child, the upper bound proves the run stays bounded and that the other checks still completed via ThreadPoolExecutor. stderr has no Traceback and stdout still contains the full inventory (a timeout must not abort the gather). No 'sleep' process from the run survives (assert by scanning the process table for the fake script path or by the elapsed bound alone if the suite forbids ps).",
   "can_fail_because": "Drop 'timeout=timeout' from subprocess.run in run_cmd \u2192 the child hangs 60s, elapsed > 25 red and the case hits the test-level timeout. Or 'CMD_TIMEOUT = 10' \u2192 '300' \u2192 detail 'no answer within 300s' red. Or delete 'except subprocess.TimeoutExpired: return \"timeout\", \u2026' \u2192 TimeoutExpired escapes, rc 1 plus traceback, inventory gone."
  },
  {
   "id": "RT-11",
   "name": "Fake qwen guard: readiness.py may only ever call `qwen --version`",
   "setup": "bin/qwen is a script that appends '$*' as one line to a log file given by an env var pointing into tmp_path, then: if argv == '--version', print 'qwen-fake 0.0' and exit 0; for ANY other argv print 'UNEXPECTED qwen INVOCATION: $*' and exit 9. This is the quota tripwire: a bare 'qwen <word>' is a one-shot prompt that spends money.",
   "action": "Four separate runs sharing one fresh log per run: markdown, --json, --require qwen, --self-test.",
   "expected": "In every run the log holds exactly one line, '--version' (a plain inventory run touches qwen exactly once \u2014 a second call per run is the regression this pins). The qwen Tools row is verified with detail 'qwen-fake 0.0' in the markdown and --json runs. 'UNEXPECTED' never appears in stdout or stderr, and no row reports 'exit 9'. --self-test exits 0 without touching qwen at all (its log is empty).",
   "can_fail_because": "Add 'run_cmd([\"qwen\", \"models\"])' beside check_agy_login, or change check_binary's probe from [name, '--version'] to [name, '--help'] / [name] \u2014 the log gains a non-'--version' line and exits 9, red. This is the single most valuable case in the file: it is what keeps the docstring promise 'no canary is run, no quota spent' mechanically true rather than aspirational."
  },
